Giovanni Maria\\n  Tomaselli, Enrico Trincherini\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Black hole superradiance with (dark) matter \
accretion\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", "\"30+21 pages, 14 figures\""}]}, {
              
               RowBox[{"\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "\"10.1103/PhysRevD.107.104018\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"gr-qc astro-ph.HE hep-ph hep-th\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]},\
 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  Studies of black hole superradiance often focus on the \
growth of a cloud in\\nisolation, accompanied by the spin-down of the black \
hole. In this paper, we\\nconsider the additional effect of the accretion of \
matter and angular momentum\\nfrom the environment. We show that, in many \
cases, the black hole evolves by\\ndrifting along the superradiance \
threshold, in which case the evolution of its\\nparameters can be described \
analytically or semi-analytically. We quantify the\\nconditions under which \
accretion can serve as a mechanism to increase the\\ncloud-to-black hole mass \
ratio, beyond the standard maximum of about 10%. This\\noccurs by a process \
we call over-superradiance, whereby accretion effectively\\nfeeds the \
superradiance cloud, by way of the black hole. We give two \
explicit\\nexamples: accretion from a vortex expected in wave dark matter and \
accretion\\nfrom a baryonic disk. In the former case, we estimate the \
accretion rate by\\nusing an analytical fit to the asymptotic behavior of the \
confluent Heun\\nfunction. Level transition, whereby one cloud level grows \
while the other\\nshrinks, can be understood in a similar way.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
