               RowBox[{"\"id\"", "\[Rule]", "\"2210.00011\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"submitter\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Jun Tsujimura\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"authors\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Jun Tsujimura and Yasusada Nambu\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Entanglement Renyi entropy of two disjoint intervals for \
large $c$\\n  Liouville field theory\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", "\"14 pages, 3 figures\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "\"10.3390/e24121758\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]},\
 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  Entanglement entropy (EE) is a quantitative measure of \
the effective degrees\\nof freedom and the correlation between the \
sub-systems of a physical system.\\nUsing the replica trick, we can obtain \
the EE by evaluating the entanglement\\nRenyi entropy (ERE). The ERE is a \
$q$-analogue of the EE and expressed by the\\n$q$ replicated partition \
function. In the semi-classical approximation, it is\\napparently easy to \
calculate the EE because the classical action represents the\\npartition \
function by the saddle point approximation and we do not need to\\nperform \
the path integral for the evaluation of the partition function. In\\nprevious \
studies, it has been assumed that only the minimal-valued saddle \
point\\ncontributes to the EE. In this paper, we propose that all the saddle \
points\\ncontribute equally to the EE by dealing carefully with the \
semi-classical limit\\nand then the $q \\\\to 1$ limit. For example, we \
numerically evaluate the ERE of\\ntwo disjoint intervals for the large $c$ \
Liouville field theory with $q \\\\sim\\n1$. We exploit the BPZ equation with \
the four twist operators, whose solution\\nis given by the Heun function. We \
determine the ERE by tuning the behavior of\\nthe Heun function such that it \
becomes consistent with the geometry of the\\nreplica manifold. We find the \
same two saddle points as previous studies for $q\\n\\\\sim 1$ in the above \
system. Then, we provide the ERE for the large but finite\\n$c$ and the $q \\\
\\sim 1$ in case that all the saddle points contribute equally to\\nthe ERE. \
Based on this work, it shall be of interest to reconsider EE in \
other\\nsemi-classical physical systems with multiple saddle \
points.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 RowBox[{"{", 
                   RowBox[{"{", 
                    RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"\"version\"", "\[Rule]", "\"v1\""}], ",", 
                    
                    RowBox[{
                    "\"created\"", "\[Rule]", 
                    "\"Fri, 30 Sep 2022 18:00:00 GMT\""}]}], "}"}], "}"}]}]}, {
