            "Jun Tsujimura and Yasusada Nambu", "title" -> 
            "Entanglement Renyi entropy of two disjoint intervals for large \
$c$\n  Liouville field theory", "comments" -> "14 pages, 3 figures", 
            "journal-ref" -> Null, "doi" -> "10.3390/e24121758", "report-no" -> 
            Null, "categories" -> "hep-th", "license" -> 
            "http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/", "abstract" -> 
            "  Entanglement entropy (EE) is a quantitative measure of the \
effective degrees\nof freedom and the correlation between the sub-systems of \
a physical system.\nUsing the replica trick, we can obtain the EE by \
evaluating the entanglement\nRenyi entropy (ERE). The ERE is a $q$-analogue \
of the EE and expressed by the\n$q$ replicated partition function. In the \
semi-classical approximation, it is\napparently easy to calculate the EE \
because the classical action represents the\npartition function by the saddle \
point approximation and we do not need to\nperform the path integral for the \
evaluation of the partition function. In\nprevious studies, it has been \
assumed that only the minimal-valued saddle point\ncontributes to the EE. In \
this paper, we propose that all the saddle points\ncontribute equally to the \
EE by dealing carefully with the semi-classical limit\nand then the $q \\to \
1$ limit. For example, we numerically evaluate the ERE of\ntwo disjoint \
intervals for the large $c$ Liouville field theory with $q \\sim\n1$. We \
exploit the BPZ equation with the four twist operators, whose solution\nis \
given by the Heun function. We determine the ERE by tuning the behavior of\n\
the Heun function such that it becomes consistent with the geometry of the\n\
replica manifold. We find the same two saddle points as previous studies for \
$q\n\\sim 1$ in the above system. Then, we provide the ERE for the large but \
finite\n$c$ and the $q \\sim 1$ in case that all the saddle points contribute \
equally to\nthe ERE. Based on this work, it shall be of interest to \
reconsider EE in other\nsemi-classical physical systems with multiple saddle \
points.\n", 
