Alexander\\n  Zhiboedov\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", "\"A thermal product formula\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"50 pages + appendices, 20 figures, typos corrected and \
citations\\n  added\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "\"CERN-TH-2023-062\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"hep-th cond-mat.stat-mech cond-mat.str-el gr-qc\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]},\
 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  We show that holographic thermal two-sided two-point \
correlators take the\\nform of a product over quasi-normal modes (QNMs). Due \
to this fact, the\\ntwo-point function admits a natural dispersive \
representation with a positive\\ndiscontinuity at the location of QNMs. We \
explore the general constraints on\\nthe structure of QNMs that follow from \
the operator product expansion, the\\npresence of the singularity inside the \
black hole, and the hydrodynamic\\nexpansion of the correlator. We illustrate \
these constraints through concrete\\nexamples. We suggest that the product \
formula for thermal correlators may hold\\nfor more general large N chaotic \
systems, and we check this hypothesis in\\nseveral models.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 RowBox[{"{", 
                   RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"{", 
                    RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"\"version\"", "\[Rule]", "\"v1\""}], ",", 
                    
