           "id" -> "2309.02482", "submitter" -> "Bruno Bucciotti", "authors" -> 
            "Bruno Bucciotti, Enrico Trincherini", "title" -> 
            "Interplay between Black Holes and Ultralight Dark Matter: \
Analytic\n  Solutions", "comments" -> 
            "29 pages + appendices, v2: minor improvements, matches journal\n \
 version", "journal-ref" -> Null, "doi" -> "10.1007/JHEP11(2023)193", 
            "report-no" -> Null, "categories" -> "hep-th astro-ph.CO gr-qc", 
            "license" -> "http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/", 
            "abstract" -> 
            "  Dark matter (DM) can consist of a scalar field so light that \
DM particles in\nthe galactic halo are best described by classical waves. We \
investigate how\nthese classical solutions are influenced by the presence of \
a non-rotating\nsupermassive black hole at the center of the galaxy, using an \
analytical,\nalbeit approximate, approach.\n  Relying on this analytic \
control, we examine the consequences of imposing\ncausal boundary conditions \
at the horizon, which are typically overlooked.\nFirst, we examine the \
scenario where the backreaction of dark matter can be\nneglected. The scalar \
field decays like a power law at large distances, thus\nendowing the black \
hole with \"hair\". We derive solutions for the field profile\nover a wide \
range of parameters, including cases with rotating dark matter. As\na \
by-product, we extract the dynamical Love numbers for scalar perturbations.\n\
Next, we determine the spectrum of bound states and their behaviour.\n  \
Finally, we incorporate the self-gravity of the scalar field, with a focus on\
\nthe situation where dark matter forms a soliton (boson star) at the center \
of\nthe galaxy. We derive an analytical expression for the soliton at every\n\
distance from the center. With a solution that remains applicable even at\n\
horizon scales, we can reliably compute the accretion rate of the black hole.\
\n", "versions" -> {{
              "version" -> "v1", "created" -> 
               "Tue, 5 Sep 2023 18:00:00 GMT"}, {
              "version" -> "v2", "created" -> 
               "Mon, 11 Dec 2023 09:43:00 GMT"}}, "update_date" -> 
            "2023-12-12", 
            "authors_parsed" -> {{"Bucciotti", "Bruno", ""}, {
              "Trincherini", "Enrico", ""}}}], "Tooltip"]& ], 
        TagBox[
         TooltipBox[
          DiskBox[{1.6327737154799589`, 1.392860364886087}, 
           0.06364453000660265],
          TagBox[
           TagBox[
            GridBox[{{
               RowBox[{"\"id\"", "\[Rule]", "\"2309.05559\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"submitter\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Felipe Diaz\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"authors\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Adolfo Cisterna, Felipe Diaz, Robert B. Mann, Julio Oliva\
\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Exploring Accelerating Hairy Black Holes in $2+1$ \
Dimensions: The\\n  Asymptotically Locally Anti-de Sitter Class and its \
Holography\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"45 pages, 17 figures, 3 tables. Published version\""}]}, {
              
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", "\"JHEP11(2023)073\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "\"10.1007/JHEP11(2023)073\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th gr-qc\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  In the realm of lower-dimensional accelerating \
spacetimes, it is\\nwell-established that the presence of domain walls, which \
are co-dimension one\\ntopological defects, is a necessary condition for \
their construction. We expand\\nthe geometric framework by adding a \
conformally coupled scalar field. This\\nendeavor leads to the identification \
of several new families of\\nthree-dimensional accelerating spacetimes with \
asymptotically locally anti-de\\nSitter (AdS) behavior. Notably, one of these \
solutions showcases a hairy\\ngeneralization of the accelerating BTZ black \
hole. This solution is constructed\\nat both slow and rapid phases of \
acceleration, and its connection with\\nestablished vacuum spacetime models \
is explicitly elucidated. The inclusion of\\nthe scalar field imparts a \
non-constant Ricci curvature to the domain wall,\\nthereby rendering these \
configurations particularly suitable for the\\nconstruction of \
two-dimensional quantum black holes. To establish a well-posed\\nvariational \
principle in the presence of the domain wall, two essential steps\\nare \
undertaken. First, we extend the conventional renormalized AdS$_3$ \
action\\nto accommodate the presence of the scalar field. Second, to \
establish a\\nwell-posed variational principle, we extend the renormalized \
AdS$_3$ action to\\ninclude the scalar field and incorporate the \
Gibbons--Hawking--York term for\\ninternal boundaries and domain wall \
tension. We engage in holographic\\ncomputations, thereby determining the \
explicit form of the holographic stress\\ntensor. In this context, the stress \
tensor can be expressed as that of a\\nperfect fluid situated on a curved \
background. Additionally, it paves the road\\nto ascertain the spacetime \
mass. Finally, we close by demonstrating the\\nexistence of three-dimensional \
accelerating spacetimes with asymptotically\\nlocally flat and asymptotically \
locally de Sitter geometries, particularly\\nthose embodying black \
holes.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
