            "  In this work we present a novel approach to the study of \
cosmological\nparticle production in asymptotically Minkowski spacetimes. We \
emphasize that\nit is possible to determine the amount of particle production \
by focusing on\nthe mathematical properties of the mode function \
equations,i.e. their\nsingularities and monodromies, sidestepping the need to \
solve those equations.\nWe consider in detail creation of scalar and spin 1/2 \
particles in four\ndimensional asymptotically Minkowski flat FLRW spacetimes. \
We explain that when\nthe mode function equation for scalar fields has only \
regular singular points,\nthe corresponding scale factors are asymptotically \
Minkowski. For Dirac spin\n1/2 fields, the requirement of mode function \
equations with only regular points\nis more restrictive, and picks up a \
subset of the aforementioned scale factors.\nFor the scalar case, we argue \
that there are two different regimes of particle\nproduction; while most of \
the literature has focused on only one of these\nregimes, the other regime \
presents enhanced particle production. On the other\nhand, for Dirac fermions \
we find a single regime of particle production.\nFinally, we very briefly \
comment on the possibility of studying particle\nproduction in spacetimes \
that don't asymptote to Minkowski, by considering mode\nfunction equations \
with irregular singular points.\n", 
