            "http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/", "abstract" -> 
            "  We present a scattering amplitude formalism to study the tidal \
heating\neffects of nonspinning neutron stars incorporating both worldline \
effective\nfield theory and relativistic stellar perturbation theory. In \
neutron stars,\ntidal heating arises from fluid viscosity due to various \
scattering processes\nin the interior. It also serves as a channel for the \
exchange of energy and\nangular momentum between the neutron star and its \
environment. In the interior\nof the neutron star, we first derive two master \
perturbation equations that\ncapture fluid perturbations accurate to linear \
order in frequency. Remarkably,\nthese equations receive no contribution from \
bulk viscosity due to a peculiar\nadiabatic incompressibility which arises in \
stellar fluid for non-barotropic\nperturbations. In the exterior, the metric \
perturbations reduce to the\nRegge-Wheeler (RW) equation which we solve using \
the analytical\nMano-Suzuki-Takasugi (MST) method. We compute the amplitude \
for gravitational\nwaves scattering off a neutron star, also known as \
gravitational Raman\nscattering. From the amplitude, we obtain expressions \
for the electric\nquadrupolar static Love number and the leading dissipation \
number to all orders\nin compactness. We then compute the leading dissipation \
number for various\nrealistic equation-of-state(s) and estimate the change in \
the number of\ngravitational wave cycles due to tidal heating during inspiral \
in the\nLIGO-Virgo-KAGRA (LVK) band.\n", 
            "versions" -> {{
              "version" -> "v1", "created" -> 
               "Thu, 11 Jul 2024 09:26:04 GMT"}, {
              "version" -> "v2", "created" -> 
               "Fri, 26 Jul 2024 17:42:14 GMT"}}, "update_date" -> 
