            "id" -> "2206.07720", "submitter" -> "Matthew Dodelson", 
             "authors" -> 
             "Matthew Dodelson, Alba Grassi, Cristoforo Iossa, Daniel Panea \
Lichtig,\n  and Alexander Zhiboedov", "title" -> 
             "Holographic thermal correlators from supersymmetric instantons",
              "comments" -> 
             "9 pages + appendices, 2 figures. v2: typos corrected, \
references\n  added", "journal-ref" -> "SciPost Phys. 14, 116 (2023)", "doi" -> 
             "10.21468/SciPostPhys.14.5.116", "report-no" -> 
             "CERN-TH-2022-095", "categories" -> 
             "hep-th gr-qc math-ph math.MP", "license" -> 
             "http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/", 
             "abstract" -> 
             "  We present an exact formula for the thermal scalar two-point \
function in\nfour-dimensional holographic conformal field theories. The \
problem of finding\nit reduces to the analysis of the wave equation on the \
AdS-Schwarzschild\nbackground. The two-point function is computed from the \
connection coefficients\nof the Heun equation, which can be expressed in \
terms of the\nNekrasov-Shatashvili partition function of an SU(2) \
supersymmetric gauge theory\nwith four fundamental hypermultiplets. The \
result is amenable to numerical\nevaluation upon truncating the number of \
instantons in the convergent expansion\nof the partition function. We also \
examine it analytically in various limits.\nAt large spin the instanton \
expansion of the thermal two-point function\ndirectly maps to the light-cone \
bootstrap analysis of the heavy-light\nfour-point function. Using this \
connection, we compute the OPE data of\nheavy-light double-twist operators. \
We compare our prediction to the\nperturbative results available in the \
literature and find perfect agreement.\n", 
             "versions" -> {{
               "version" -> "v1", "created" -> 
                "Wed, 15 Jun 2022 18:00:01 GMT"}, {
               "version" -> "v2", "created" -> 
                "Fri, 16 Sep 2022 18:20:34 GMT"}}, "update_date" -> 
