          Annotation[#, 
           
           TableForm[{
            "id" -> "2208.06408", "submitter" -> "Giovanni Maria Tomaselli", 
             "authors" -> 
             "Lam Hui, Y.T. Albert Law, Luca Santoni, Guanhao Sun, Giovanni \
Maria\n  Tomaselli, Enrico Trincherini", "title" -> 
             "Black hole superradiance with (dark) matter accretion", 
             "comments" -> "30+21 pages, 14 figures", "journal-ref" -> Null, 
             "doi" -> "10.1103/PhysRevD.107.104018", "report-no" -> Null, 
             "categories" -> "gr-qc astro-ph.HE hep-ph hep-th", "license" -> 
             "http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/", 
             "abstract" -> 
             "  Studies of black hole superradiance often focus on the growth \
of a cloud in\nisolation, accompanied by the spin-down of the black hole. In \
this paper, we\nconsider the additional effect of the accretion of matter and \
angular momentum\nfrom the environment. We show that, in many cases, the \
black hole evolves by\ndrifting along the superradiance threshold, in which \
case the evolution of its\nparameters can be described analytically or \
semi-analytically. We quantify the\nconditions under which accretion can \
serve as a mechanism to increase the\ncloud-to-black hole mass ratio, beyond \
the standard maximum of about 10%. This\noccurs by a process we call \
over-superradiance, whereby accretion effectively\nfeeds the superradiance \
cloud, by way of the black hole. We give two explicit\nexamples: accretion \
from a vortex expected in wave dark matter and accretion\nfrom a baryonic \
disk. In the former case, we estimate the accretion rate by\nusing an \
analytical fit to the asymptotic behavior of the confluent Heun\nfunction. \
Level transition, whereby one cloud level grows while the other\nshrinks, can \
be understood in a similar way.\n", 
             "versions" -> {{
               "version" -> "v1", "created" -> 
                "Fri, 12 Aug 2022 17:59:58 GMT"}, {
               "version" -> "v2", "created" -> 
                "Fri, 27 Jan 2023 15:28:44 GMT"}, {
