theory of a system\nprobing a noisy thermal environment, distilling lessons \
learnt from previous\nholographic analyses. Our treatment is applicable both \
when the system is\ncoupled to short-lived (Markovian), and long-lived \
(non-Markovian)\nenvironmental degrees of freedom. The thermal environment is \
modeled using an\nasymptotically AdS black hole, and the systems of interest \
are simple probe\nfield theories. The effective stochastic dynamics of the \
system is governed by\nreal-time thermal correlators, which we compute using \
the gravitational\nSchwinger-Keldysh (grSK) geometry. We describe the \
structure of arbitrary\ntree-level contact and exchange Witten diagrams in \
the grSK geometry. In\nparticular, we argue, that all such diagrams reduce to \
integrals supported on a\nsingle copy of the exterior of the black hole. The \
integrand is obtained as a\nmultiple discontinuity of a function comprising \
ingoing boundary-bulk\npropagators, monodromy functions which appear as \
radial Boltzmann weights, and\nvertex factors. These results allow us to \
deduce the analytic structure of\nreal-time thermal n-point functions in \
holographic CFTs. We illustrate the\ngeneral statements by a two-dimensional \
toy model, dual to fields in the BTZ\nbackground, which we argue captures \
many of the essential features of generic\nopen holographic QFTs.\n", 
             "versions" -> {{
