                RowBox[{
                 "\"submitter\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Mukund Rangamani\""}]}, {
                
                RowBox[{
                 "\"authors\"", "\[Rule]", 
                  "\"R. Loganayagam, Mukund Rangamani, Julio Virrueta\""}]}, {
               
                
                RowBox[{
                 "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                  "\"Holographic thermal correlators: A tale of Fuchsian ODEs \
and integration\\n  contours\""}]}, {
                
                RowBox[{
                 "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", 
                  "\"35 pages plus appendices. v2: improvements in \
presentation. published\\n  version\""}]}, {
                RowBox[{"\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
                
                RowBox[{
                 "\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "\"10.1007/JHEP07(2023)008\""}]}, {
                RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
                
                RowBox[{
                 "\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", 
                  "\"hep-th gr-qc math-ph math.MP\""}]}, {
                
                RowBox[{
                 "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                  "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]}\
, {
                
                RowBox[{
                 "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                  "\"  We analyze real-time thermal correlation functions of \
conserved currents in\\nholographic field theories using the grSK geometry, \
which provides a contour\\nprescription for their evaluation. We demonstrate \
its efficacy, arguing that\\nthere are situations involving components of \
conserved currents, or derivative\\ninteractions, where such a prescription \
is, in fact, essential. To this end, we\\nfirst undertake a careful analysis \
of the linearized wave equations in AdS\\nblack hole backgrounds and identify \
the ramification points of the solutions as\\na function of (complexified) \
frequency and momentum. All the equations we study\\nare Fuchsian with only \
regular singular points that for the most part are\\nassociated with the \
geometric features of the background. Special features,\\ne.g., the \
appearance of apparent singular points at the horizon, whence\\noutgoing \
solutions end up being analytic, arise at higher codimension loci \
in\\nparameter space. Using the grSK geometry, we demonstrate that these \
apparent\\nsingularities do not correspond to any interesting physical \
features in\\nhigher-point functions. We also argue that the \
Schwinger-Keldysh collapse and\\nKMS conditions, implemented by the grSK \
geometry, continue to hold even in the\\npresence of such singularities. For \
charged black holes above a critical\\ncharge, the energy density operator \
does not possess an exponentially growing\\nmode, associated with \
`pole-skipping' (from one such apparent singularity). Our\\nanalysis suggests \
that the connection between the scrambling physics of black\\nholes and \
energy transport has, at best, a limited domain of validity.\\n\""}]}, {
                RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
                  RowBox[{"{", 
                    RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"{", 
