           "id" -> "2303.04175", "submitter" -> "Pratik Nandy", "authors" -> 
            "Aranya Bhattacharya, Pratik Nandy, Pingal Pratyush Nath, \
Himanshu Sahu", "title" -> 
            "On Krylov complexity in open systems: an approach via bi-Lanczos\
\n  algorithm", "comments" -> 
            "v2: some sections added and updated, clarifications added, \
published\n  version in JHEP", "journal-ref" -> "JHEP 12 (2023) 066", "doi" -> 
            "10.1007/JHEP12(2023)066", "report-no" -> "YITP-23-25", 
            "categories" -> 
            "quant-ph cond-mat.stat-mech cond-mat.str-el hep-th", "license" -> 
            "http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/", "abstract" -> 
            "  Continuing the previous initiatives arXiv: 2207.05347 and \
arXiv: 2212.06180,\nwe pursue the exploration of operator growth and Krylov \
complexity in\ndissipative open quantum systems. In this paper, we resort to \
the bi-Lanczos\nalgorithm generating two bi-orthogonal Krylov spaces, which \
individually\ngenerate non-orthogonal subspaces. Unlike the previously \
studied Arnoldi\niteration, this algorithm renders the Lindbladian into a \
purely tridiagonal\nform, thus opening up a possibility to study a wide class \
of dissipative\nintegrable and chaotic systems by computing Krylov complexity \
at late times.\nOur study relies on two specific systems, the dissipative \
transverse-field\nIsing model (TFIM) and the dissipative interacting XXZ \
chain. We find that, for\nthe weak coupling, initial Lanczos coefficients can \
efficiently distinguish\nintegrable and chaotic evolution before the \
dissipative effect sets in, which\nresults in more fluctuations in higher \
Lanczos coefficients. This results in\nthe equal saturation of late-time \
complexity for both integrable and chaotic\ncases, making the notion of \
late-time chaos dubious.\n", 
            "versions" -> {{
              "version" -> "v1", "created" -> 
               "Tue, 7 Mar 2023 19:00:18 GMT"}, {
              "version" -> "v2", "created" -> 
               "Thu, 14 Dec 2023 09:36:05 GMT"}}, "update_date" -> 
            "2023-12-15", 
