                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]},\
 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  Due to superradiant instabilities, clouds of ultralight \
bosons can\\nspontaneously grow around rotating black holes, creating \
so-called\\n\\\"gravitational atoms\\\". In this work, we study their \
dynamical effects on binary\\nsystems. We first focus on open orbits, showing \
that the presence of a cloud\\ncan increase the cross section for the \
dynamical capture of a compact object by\\nmore than an order of magnitude. \
We then consider closed orbits and demonstrate\\nthat the backreaction of the \
cloud's ionization on the orbital motion should be\\nidentified as dynamical \
friction. Finally, we study for the first time\\neccentric and inclined \
orbits. We find that, while ionization quickly\\ncircularizes the binary, it \
barely affects the inclination angle. These results\\nenable a more realistic \
description of the dynamics of gravitational atoms in\\nbinaries and pave the \
way for dedicated searches with future gravitational \
wave\\ndetectors.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
