            "  I study the fermionic $U(N)$ Gross-Neveu model at imaginary \
chemical\npotential and finite temperature for odd $d$ dimensions, in the \
strong coupling\nregime, by using the gap (saddle point) equation for the \
fermion condensate of\nthe model. This equation describes the phase \
transitions from weak to strong\ncoupling regime. I point out that the higher \
odd dimensional gap equations are\nlinear combinations of the lower \
dimensional equations in a way that as the\ndimension of the model increases \
the lower dimensions are weaker coupled but\nstill in the strong coupling \
regime. Interestingly, at a specific value of the\nchemical potential, \
exactly in the middle of the thermal windows that separate\nthe fermionic \
from the bosonic (condensed) state of the fermions, I find the\nmass of the \
fermion condensate for $d=3,5,7,9$. An anomaly occurs at the $5$\ndimensional \
theory where it is stronger coupled against other theories in\nhigher \
dimensions and lower energy. The main idea of this work is that the\ncut-off \
$\\Lambda$ regulator for the UV divergent parts of the fermion mass\nsaddle \
point equation, plays the role of a physical parameter. This idea is\nbased \
on the identity of the asymptotic freedom of the Gross-Neveu model as a\ntoy \
model for QCD.\n", 
