large\n  spin", "comments" -> 
             "46 pages, 8 figures, some more typos corrected", "journal-ref" -> 
             Null, "doi" -> Null, "report-no" -> Null, "categories" -> 
             "hep-th cond-mat.stat-mech cond-mat.str-el", "license" -> 
             "http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/", "abstract" -> 
             "  We apply the OPE inversion formula on thermal two-point \
functions of fermions\nto obtain thermal one-point function of fermion \
bi-linears appearing in the\ncorresponding OPE. We primarily focus on the OPE \
channel which contains the\nstress tensor of the theory. We apply our \
formalism to the mean field theory of\nfermions and verify that the inversion \
formula reproduces the spectrum as well\nas their corresponding thermal \
one-point functions. We then examine the large\n$N$ critical Gross-Neveu \
model in $d=2k+1$ dimensions with $k$ even and at\nfinite temperature. We \
show that stress tensor evaluated from the inversion\nformula agrees with \
that evaluated from the partition function at the critical\npoint. We \
demonstrate the expectation values of 3 different classes of higher\nspin \
currents are all related to each other by numerical constants, spin and\nthe \
thermal mass. We evaluate the ratio of the thermal expectation values of\n\
higher spin currents at the critical point to the Gaussian fixed point or the\
\nStefan-Boltzmann result, both for the large $N$ critical $O(N)$ model and \
the\nGross-Neveu model in odd dimensions. This ratio is always less than one \
and it\napproaches unity on increasing the spin with the dimension $d$ held \
fixed. The\nratio however approaches zero when the dimension $d$ is increased \
with the spin\nheld fixed.\n", 
             "versions" -> {{
