              "Shetye", "Omkar", ""}}}], "Tooltip"]& ], 
        TagBox[
         TooltipBox[
          DiskBox[{5.038648367633575, 4.355880853554698}, 0.08283829248930569],
          TagBox[
           TagBox[
            GridBox[{{
               RowBox[{"\"id\"", "\[Rule]", "\"2309.07645\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"submitter\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Dimitrios Zoakos\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{
                "\"authors\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"George Georgiou and Dimitrios Zoakos\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Holographic three-point correlators at finite density and \
temperature\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", "\"36 pages, 5 figures\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/\""}]}, {
              
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  We calculate holographically three-point functions of \
scalar operators with\\nlarge dimensions at finite density and finite \
temperature. To achieve this, we\\nconstruct new solutions that involve two \
isometries of the deformed internal\\nspace. The novel feature of these \
solutions is that the corresponding two-point\\nfunction depends not only on \
the conformal dimension but also on the difference\\nbetween the two angular \
momenta. After identifying the dual operators, we\\nsystematically calculate \
three-point correlators as an expansion in powers of\\nthe temperature and \
the chemical potential. Our analytic perturbative results\\nare in agreement \
with the exact numerical computation. The three point\\ncorrelator (when the \
background contains either temperature or density but not\\nboth) is always a \
monotonic function of the temperature or the chemical\\npotential. However, \
when both parameters are present the three point correlator\\nis no longer a \
monotonic function. For fixed finite temperature and small\\nvalues of the \
chemical potential a minimum of the three-point function \
appears.\\nSurprisingly, contributions from the internal space do not depend \
on the\\nchemical potential or the temperature, as long as those are treated \
as\\nperturbations.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 RowBox[{"{", 
                   RowBox[{"{", 
                    RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"\"version\"", "\[Rule]", "\"v1\""}], ",", 
                    
                    RowBox[{
                    "\"created\"", "\[Rule]", 
                    "\"Thu, 14 Sep 2023 12:10:39 GMT\""}]}], "}"}], "}"}]}]}, {
