           "id" -> "2309.07645", "submitter" -> "Dimitrios Zoakos", "authors" -> 
            "George Georgiou and Dimitrios Zoakos", "title" -> 
            "Holographic three-point correlators at finite density and \
temperature", "comments" -> "36 pages, 5 figures", "journal-ref" -> Null, 
            "doi" -> Null, "report-no" -> Null, "categories" -> "hep-th", 
            "license" -> "http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/", 
            "abstract" -> 
            "  We calculate holographically three-point functions of scalar \
operators with\nlarge dimensions at finite density and finite temperature. To \
achieve this, we\nconstruct new solutions that involve two isometries of the \
deformed internal\nspace. The novel feature of these solutions is that the \
corresponding two-point\nfunction depends not only on the conformal dimension \
but also on the difference\nbetween the two angular momenta. After \
identifying the dual operators, we\nsystematically calculate three-point \
correlators as an expansion in powers of\nthe temperature and the chemical \
potential. Our analytic perturbative results\nare in agreement with the exact \
numerical computation. The three point\ncorrelator (when the background \
contains either temperature or density but not\nboth) is always a monotonic \
function of the temperature or the chemical\npotential. However, when both \
parameters are present the three point correlator\nis no longer a monotonic \
function. For fixed finite temperature and small\nvalues of the chemical \
potential a minimum of the three-point function appears.\nSurprisingly, \
contributions from the internal space do not depend on the\nchemical \
potential or the temperature, as long as those are treated as\nperturbations.\
\n", "versions" -> {{
