                RowBox[{
                 "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                  "\"  Recent works have suggested that nonlinear (quadratic) \
effects in black hole\\nperturbation theory may be important for describing a \
black hole ringdown. We\\nshow that the technique of uniform approximations \
can be used to accurately\\ncompute 1) nonlinear amplitudes at large \
distances in terms of the linear ones,\\n2) linear (and nonlinear) \
quasi-normal mode frequencies, 3) the wavefunction\\nfor both linear and \
nonlinear modes. Our method can be seen as a generalization\\nof the WKB \
approximation, with the advantages of not losing accuracy at large\\novertone \
number and not requiring matching conditions. To illustrate \
the\\neffectiveness of this method we consider a simplified source for \
the\\nsecond-order Zerilli equation, which we use to numerically compute \
the\\namplitude of nonlinear modes for a range of values of the angular \
momentum\\nnumber.\\n\""}]}, {
                RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
