               RowBox[{"\"id\"", "\[Rule]", "\"2310.09839\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"submitter\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Keisuke Nakashi\""}]}, {
              
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"authors\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Keisuke Nakashi, Masashi Kimura, Hayato Motohashi, \
Kazufumi Takahashi\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Black hole ringdown from physically sensible initial \
value problem in\\n  higher-order scalar-tensor theories\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", "\"36 pages, 11 figures\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Phys. Rev. D109, no.2, 024034 (2024)\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "\"10.1103/PhysRevD.109.024034\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{
                "\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "\"RUP-23-19, YITP-23-118\""}]}, {
              
               RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"gr-qc hep-th\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  We study odd-parity perturbations about static and \
spherically symmetric\\nblack hole solutions with a linearly time-dependent \
scalar field in\\nhigher-order scalar-tensor theories. In particular, we \
consider stealth\\nSchwarzschild and stealth Schwarzschild-de Sitter \
solutions, where the\\ndeviation from the general relativity case is \
controlled by a single parameter.\\nWe find that complex frequencies of \
quasinormal modes (QNMs) are given by a\\nsimple scaling of those in general \
relativity. We also show that there is a\\ndegeneracy between the parameter \
characterizing the modification from general\\nrelativity and the black hole \
mass. We then consider a physically sensible\\ninitial value problem by \
taking into account the fact that the effective metric\\nfor the odd-parity \
perturbations is in general different from the background\\nmetric. We \
confirm that damped oscillations appearing at late times are \
indeed\\ndominated by the QNMs. Our analysis includes the case where the \
perturbations\\nare superluminal, and we demonstrate in this case that the \
perturbations can\\nescape from the region inside the horizon for the \
background metric.\\n\""}]}, {
