               RowBox[{"\"id\"", "\[Rule]", "\"2310.13047\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"submitter\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Pietro Pelliconi\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"authors\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Pietro Pelliconi, Julian Sonner\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"The Influence Functional in open holography: entanglement \
and R\\\\'enyi\\n  entropies\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"43 pages, 9 figures. v2: references added. v3: improved \
presentation\\n  on different prescriptions to compute the replicated \
Influence Functional;\\n  clarified the relation with coarse graining; \
references added. Matching the\\n  published version\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"hep-th cond-mat.stat-mech quant-ph\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  Open quantum systems are defined as ordinary unitary \
quantum theories coupled\\nto a set of external degrees of freedom, which are \
introduced to take on the\\nr\\\\^ole of an unobserved environment. Here we \
study examples of open quantum\\nfield theories, with the aid of the \
so-called Feynman- Vernon Influence\\nFunctional (IF), including field \
theories that arise in holographic duality. We\\ninterpret the system in the \
presence of an IF as an open effective field\\ntheory, able to capture the \
effect of the unobserved environment. Our main\\nfocus is on computing \
R\\\\'enyi and entanglement entropies in such systems, whose\\ndescription \
from the IF, or \\\"open EFT\\\", point of view we develop in this \
paper.\\nThe issue of computing the entanglement-R\\\\'enyi entropies in open \
quantum\\nsystems is surprisingly rich, and we point out how different \
prescriptions for\\nthe IF may be appropriate depending on the application of \
choice. A striking\\napplication of our methods concerns the fine-grained \
entropy of subsystems when\\nincluding gravity in the setup, for example when \
considering the Hawking\\nradiation emitted by black holes. In this case we \
show that one prescription\\nfor the IF leads to answers consistent with \
unitary evolution, while the other\\nmerely reproduces standard EFT results, \
well known to be inconsistent with\\nunitary global evolution. We establish \
these results for asymptotically AdS\\ngravity in arbitrary dimensions, and \
illustrate them with explicit analytical\\nexpressions for the IF in the case \
of matter-coupled JT gravity in two\\ndimensions.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
