            "43 pages, 9 figures. v2: references added. v3: improved \
presentation\n  on different prescriptions to compute the replicated \
Influence Functional;\n  clarified the relation with coarse graining; \
references added. Matching the\n  published version", "journal-ref" -> Null, 
            "doi" -> Null, "report-no" -> Null, "categories" -> 
            "hep-th cond-mat.stat-mech quant-ph", "license" -> 
            "http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/", "abstract" -> 
            "  Open quantum systems are defined as ordinary unitary quantum \
theories coupled\nto a set of external degrees of freedom, which are \
introduced to take on the\nr\\^ole of an unobserved environment. Here we \
study examples of open quantum\nfield theories, with the aid of the so-called \
Feynman- Vernon Influence\nFunctional (IF), including field theories that \
arise in holographic duality. We\ninterpret the system in the presence of an \
IF as an open effective field\ntheory, able to capture the effect of the \
unobserved environment. Our main\nfocus is on computing R\\'enyi and \
entanglement entropies in such systems, whose\ndescription from the IF, or \
\"open EFT\", point of view we develop in this paper.\nThe issue of computing \
the entanglement-R\\'enyi entropies in open quantum\nsystems is surprisingly \
rich, and we point out how different prescriptions for\nthe IF may be \
appropriate depending on the application of choice. A striking\napplication \
of our methods concerns the fine-grained entropy of subsystems when\n\
including gravity in the setup, for example when considering the Hawking\n\
radiation emitted by black holes. In this case we show that one prescription\n\
for the IF leads to answers consistent with unitary evolution, while the \
other\nmerely reproduces standard EFT results, well known to be inconsistent \
with\nunitary global evolution. We establish these results for asymptotically \
AdS\ngravity in arbitrary dimensions, and illustrate them with explicit \
analytical\nexpressions for the IF in the case of matter-coupled JT gravity \
in two\ndimensions.\n", 
