               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Operator dynamics in Lindbladian SYK: a Krylov complexity \
perspective\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"v2: minor edits, typos corrected, published version in \
JHEP\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", "\"JHEP 01 (2024) 094\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "\"10.1007/JHEP01(2024)094\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"YITP-23-133, RIKEN-iTHEMS-Report-23\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"quant-ph cond-mat.stat-mech cond-mat.str-el hep-th\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]},\
 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  We use Krylov complexity to study operator growth in \
the $q$-body dissipative\\nSYK model, where the dissipation is modeled by \
linear and random $p$-body\\nLindblad operators. In the large $q$ limit, we \
analytically establish the\\nlinear growth of two sets of coefficients for \
any generic jump operators. We\\nnumerically verify this by implementing the \
bi-Lanczos algorithm, which\\ntransforms the Lindbladian into a pure \
tridiagonal form. We find that the\\nKrylov complexity saturates inversely \
with the dissipation strength, while the\\ndissipative timescale grows \
logarithmically. This is akin to the behavior of\\nother \
$\\\\mathfrak{q}$-complexity measures, namely out-of-time-order \
correlator\\n(OTOC) and operator size, which we also demonstrate. We connect \
these\\nobservations to continuous quantum measurement processes. We \
further\\ninvestigate the pole structure of a generic auto-correlation and \
the\\nhigh-frequency behavior of the spectral function in the presence \
of\\ndissipation, thereby revealing a general principle for operator growth \
in\\ndissipative quantum chaotic systems.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
