            "  Quantum black holes (BHs), thought of as an excited multilevel \
system, can be\neffectively modelled by replacing an event horizon with a \
partially reflective\nmembrane. This emergent feature affects their \
interaction with hosting\nenvironments, with the most pronounced effects \
happening for environments of\nparticles with mass~$m_{\\rm p}\\lesssim (10\n\
M_{\\odot}/M)\\,10^{-11}\\mathrm{\\,eV/c^2}$, with~$M$ the BH mass. We show \
that\nultra-light bosons -- a viable dark matter candidate -- can be used to \
probe\nthe quantum nature of BHs. We derive analytical expressions for the \
accretion\nrate and dynamical friction acting on exotic compact objects \
moving through an\nultra-light scalar field, which we then use to estimate \
the orbital de-phasing\nin the inspiralling of different Laser Inteferometer \
Space Antenna (LISA)\nbinaries. Our results indicate that LISA may be able to \
discriminate quantum\nfrom classical BHs.\n", 
