               RowBox[{"\"id\"", "\[Rule]", "\"2312.12515\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"submitter\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Vasco Gennari\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"authors\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Vasco Gennari, Gregorio Carullo and Walter Del \
Pozzo\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Searching for ringdown higher modes with a numerical \
relativity-informed\\n  post-merger model\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", "\"38 pages, 14 figures\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Eur. Phys. J. C 84, 233 (2024)\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"10.1140/epjc/s10052-024-12550-x\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"gr-qc\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]},\
 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  Robust measurements of multiple black hole vibrational \
modes provide a unique\\nopportunity to characterise gravity in extreme \
curvature and dynamical regimes,\\nto better investigate the nature of \
compact objects and search for signs of new\\nphysics. We use a \
numerically-tuned quasicircular non-precessing ringdown\\nmodel, \
$\\\\texttt{TEOBPM}$, and the $\\\\texttt{pyRing}$ analysis infrastructure to\
\\nperform a time-domain spectroscopic analysis of the third catalog of \
transient\\ngravitational-wave signals, GWTC-3, searching for higher angular \
modes. The\\n$\\\\texttt{TEOBPM}$ model effectively includes non-linearities \
in the early\\npost-merger signal portion, and carries information about the \
progenitors\\nparameters through time-dependent excitation amplitudes of the \
black hole\\nquasinormal modes. Such a strategy allows us to accurately model \
the full\\npost-merger emission, recovering higher signal-to-noise ratios \
compared to\\ntemplates based on more agnostic superpositions of \
damped-sinusoids. We find\\nweak evidence for the presence of $(l,m)=(3,3)$ \
[$(l,m)=(2,1)$] mode in several\\nevents, with the largest Bayes factor in \
favour of this mode being\\n$\\\\mathcal{B}\\\\simeq 2.6$ \
[$\\\\mathcal{B}\\\\simeq 1.2$] within the peak time\\ndistribution support. \
For GW190521, we observe $\\\\mathcal{B}\\\\simeq 5.1$, but\\nonly for times \
outside the peak time support reconstructed using the highly\\naccurate \
$\\\\texttt{NRSur7dq4}$ model, indicating significant systematics\\naffecting \
such putative detection. Allowing for deviations from general\\nrelativity \
under the assumption of the presence of two modes, we find \
tentative\\nsupport for the Kerr \\\"final state conjecture\\\". Our work \
showcases a systematic\\nmethodology to robustly identify and characterise \
higher angular modes in\\nringdown-only signals, highlighting the significant \
impact of modelling\\nassumptions and peak time uncertainty on spectroscopic \
measurements, at current\\nsignal-to-noise ratios.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 RowBox[{"{", 
                   RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"{", 
