         Annotation[#, 
          
          TableForm[{
           "id" -> "2312.12515", "submitter" -> "Vasco Gennari", "authors" -> 
            "Vasco Gennari, Gregorio Carullo and Walter Del Pozzo", "title" -> 
            "Searching for ringdown higher modes with a numerical \
relativity-informed\n  post-merger model", "comments" -> 
            "38 pages, 14 figures", "journal-ref" -> 
            "Eur. Phys. J. C 84, 233 (2024)", "doi" -> 
            "10.1140/epjc/s10052-024-12550-x", "report-no" -> Null, 
            "categories" -> "gr-qc", "license" -> 
            "http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/", "abstract" -> 
            "  Robust measurements of multiple black hole vibrational modes \
provide a unique\nopportunity to characterise gravity in extreme curvature \
and dynamical regimes,\nto better investigate the nature of compact objects \
and search for signs of new\nphysics. We use a numerically-tuned \
quasicircular non-precessing ringdown\nmodel, $\\texttt{TEOBPM}$, and the \
$\\texttt{pyRing}$ analysis infrastructure to\nperform a time-domain \
spectroscopic analysis of the third catalog of transient\ngravitational-wave \
signals, GWTC-3, searching for higher angular modes. The\n$\\texttt{TEOBPM}$ \
model effectively includes non-linearities in the early\npost-merger signal \
portion, and carries information about the progenitors\nparameters through \
time-dependent excitation amplitudes of the black hole\nquasinormal modes. \
Such a strategy allows us to accurately model the full\npost-merger emission, \
recovering higher signal-to-noise ratios compared to\ntemplates based on more \
agnostic superpositions of damped-sinusoids. We find\nweak evidence for the \
presence of $(l,m)=(3,3)$ [$(l,m)=(2,1)$] mode in several\nevents, with the \
largest Bayes factor in favour of this mode being\n$\\mathcal{B}\\simeq 2.6$ \
[$\\mathcal{B}\\simeq 1.2$] within the peak time\ndistribution support. For \
GW190521, we observe $\\mathcal{B}\\simeq 5.1$, but\nonly for times outside \
the peak time support reconstructed using the highly\naccurate \
$\\texttt{NRSur7dq4}$ model, indicating significant systematics\naffecting \
such putative detection. Allowing for deviations from general\nrelativity \
under the assumption of the presence of two modes, we find tentative\nsupport \
for the Kerr \"final state conjecture\". Our work showcases a systematic\n\
methodology to robustly identify and characterise higher angular modes in\n\
ringdown-only signals, highlighting the significant impact of modelling\n\
assumptions and peak time uncertainty on spectroscopic measurements, at \
current\nsignal-to-noise ratios.\n", 
            "versions" -> {{
              "version" -> "v1", "created" -> 
               "Tue, 19 Dec 2023 19:00:04 GMT"}, {
              "version" -> "v2", "created" -> 
               "Wed, 6 Mar 2024 13:42:49 GMT"}}, "update_date" -> 
            "2024-03-07", 
            "authors_parsed" -> {{"Gennari", "Vasco", ""}, {
              "Carullo", "Gregorio", ""}, {"Del Pozzo", "Walter", ""}}}], 
          "Tooltip"]& ], 
