               RowBox[{"\"submitter\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Chethan Krishnan\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"authors\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Vaibhav Burman, Suchetan Das, Chethan Krishnan\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"A Smooth Horizon without a Smooth Horizon\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"V2: JHEP version + very minor improvements, v3: Clearer \
discussion\\n  about (averages over) eigenstates vs microcanonical typical \
states. Results\\n  and conclusions unchanged\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]},\
 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  Recent observations on type III algebras in AdS/CFT \
raise the possibility\\nthat smoothness of the black hole horizon is an \
emergent feature of the\\nlarge-$N$ limit. In this paper, we present a $bulk$ \
model for the finite-$N$\\nmechanism underlying this transition. We quantize \
a free scalar field on a BTZ\\nblack hole with a Planckian stretched horizon \
placed as a Dirichlet boundary\\nfor the field. This is a tractable model for \
the stretched horizon that does\\nnot ignore the angular directions, and it \
defines a black hole vacuum which has\\nsimilarities to (but is distinct \
from) the Boulware state. Using analytic\\napproximations for the normal \
modes, we first improve upon 't Hooft's brick\\nwall calculation: we are able \
to match $both$ the entropy and the temperature,\\n$exactly$. Emboldened by \
this, we compute the boundary Wightman function of the\\nscalar field in a \
typical pure state built on our stretched horizon vacuum, at\\nan energy \
sliver at the mass of the black hole. A key result is that despite\\nthe \
manifest lack of smoothness, this single-sided pure state \
calculation\\nyields precisely the Hartle-Hawking thermal correlator \
associated to the smooth\\nhorizon, in the small-$G_N$ limit. At finite \
$G_N$, there are variance\\ncorrections that are suppressed as \
$\\\\mathcal{O}(e^{-S_{BH}/2})$. They become\\nimportant at late times and \
resolve Maldacena's information paradox. Highly\\nexcited typical pure states \
on the stretched horizon vacuum are therefore\\nmodels for black hole \
microstates, while the smooth horizon describes the\\nthermal state. We note \
that heavy excited states on the stretched horizon are\\nbetter defined than \
the vacuum itself. These results suggest that\\ncomplementarity in the bulk \
EFT could arise from a UV complete bulk description\\nin which the black hole \
interior is not manifest.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 RowBox[{"{", 
                   RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"{", 
                    RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"\"version\"", "\[Rule]", "\"v1\""}], ",", 
                    
                    RowBox[{
                    "\"created\"", "\[Rule]", 
                    "\"Thu, 21 Dec 2023 18:32:06 GMT\""}]}], "}"}], ",", 
                    RowBox[{"{", 
                    RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"\"version\"", "\[Rule]", "\"v2\""}], ",", 
                    
                    RowBox[{
                    "\"created\"", "\[Rule]", 
                    "\"Tue, 5 Mar 2024 08:51:32 GMT\""}]}], "}"}], ",", 
                    RowBox[{"{", 
                    RowBox[{
