            "  Recent observations on type III algebras in AdS/CFT raise the \
possibility\nthat smoothness of the black hole horizon is an emergent feature \
of the\nlarge-$N$ limit. In this paper, we present a $bulk$ model for the \
finite-$N$\nmechanism underlying this transition. We quantize a free scalar \
field on a BTZ\nblack hole with a Planckian stretched horizon placed as a \
Dirichlet boundary\nfor the field. This is a tractable model for the \
stretched horizon that does\nnot ignore the angular directions, and it \
defines a black hole vacuum which has\nsimilarities to (but is distinct from) \
the Boulware state. Using analytic\napproximations for the normal modes, we \
first improve upon 't Hooft's brick\nwall calculation: we are able to match \
$both$ the entropy and the temperature,\n$exactly$. Emboldened by this, we \
compute the boundary Wightman function of the\nscalar field in a typical pure \
state built on our stretched horizon vacuum, at\nan energy sliver at the mass \
of the black hole. A key result is that despite\nthe manifest lack of \
smoothness, this single-sided pure state calculation\nyields precisely the \
Hartle-Hawking thermal correlator associated to the smooth\nhorizon, in the \
small-$G_N$ limit. At finite $G_N$, there are variance\ncorrections that are \
suppressed as $\\mathcal{O}(e^{-S_{BH}/2})$. They become\nimportant at late \
times and resolve Maldacena's information paradox. Highly\nexcited typical \
pure states on the stretched horizon vacuum are therefore\nmodels for black \
hole microstates, while the smooth horizon describes the\nthermal state. We \
note that heavy excited states on the stretched horizon are\nbetter defined \
than the vacuum itself. These results suggest that\ncomplementarity in the \
bulk EFT could arise from a UV complete bulk description\nin which the black \
hole interior is not manifest.\n", 
