, {
                
                RowBox[{
                 "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                  "\"  In this article, we demonstrate how black hole \
quasi-normal modes can emerge\\nfrom a Dirichlet brickwall model normal \
modes. We consider a probe scalar field\\nin a BTZ geometry with a Dirichlet \
brickwall and demonstrate that as the wall\\napproaches the event horizon, \
the corresponding poles in the retarded\\ncorrelator become dense and yield \
an effective branch-cut. The associated\\ndiscontinuity of the correlator \
carries the information of the black hole\\nquasi-normal modes. We further \
demonstrate that a non-vanishing angular\\nmomentum non-perturbatively \
enhances the pole-condensing. We hypothesize that\\nit is also related to \
quantum chaotic features of the corresponding spectral\\nform factor, which \
has been observed earlier. Finally, we discuss the\\nunderlying algebraic \
justification of this approximate thermalization in terms\\nof the trace of \
the algebra.\\n\""}]}, {
                RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
                  RowBox[{"{", 
