             "title" -> "Brickwall, Normal Modes and Emerging Thermality", 
             "comments" -> Null, "journal-ref" -> Null, "doi" -> Null, 
             "report-no" -> Null, "categories" -> "hep-th gr-qc", "license" -> 
             "http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/", 
             "abstract" -> 
             "  In this article, we demonstrate how black hole quasi-normal \
modes can emerge\nfrom a Dirichlet brickwall model normal modes. We consider \
a probe scalar field\nin a BTZ geometry with a Dirichlet brickwall and \
demonstrate that as the wall\napproaches the event horizon, the corresponding \
poles in the retarded\ncorrelator become dense and yield an effective \
branch-cut. The associated\ndiscontinuity of the correlator carries the \
information of the black hole\nquasi-normal modes. We further demonstrate \
that a non-vanishing angular\nmomentum non-perturbatively enhances the \
pole-condensing. We hypothesize that\nit is also related to quantum chaotic \
features of the corresponding spectral\nform factor, which has been observed \
earlier. Finally, we discuss the\nunderlying algebraic justification of this \
approximate thermalization in terms\nof the trace of the algebra.\n", 
