               RowBox[{"\"id\"", "\[Rule]", "\"2402.07977\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"submitter\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Zipeng Wang\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"authors\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Zipeng Wang, Thomas Helfer, Dina Traykova, Katy Clough, \
Emanuele Berti\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Gravitational Magnus effect from scalar dark \
matter\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", "\"10 pages, 6 figures\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{
                "\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"gr-qc astro-ph.HE\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]},\
 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  In fluid dynamics, the Magnus effect is the force \
perpendicular to the motion\\nof a spinning object as it moves through a \
medium. In general relativity, an\\nanalogous effect exists for a spinning \
compact object moving through matter,\\npurely as a result of gravitational \
interactions. In this work we consider a\\nKerr black hole moving at \
relativistic velocities through scalar dark matter\\nthat is at rest. We \
simulate the system numerically and extract the total\\nspin-curvature force \
on the black hole perpendicular to its motion. We confirm\\nthat the force \
scales linearly with the dimensionless spin parameter $a/M$ of\\nthe black \
hole up to $a/M = 0.99$, and measure its dependence on the speed $v$\\nof the \
black hole in the range $0.1 \\\\le v \\\\le 0.55$ for a fixed spin. Compared\
\\nto previous analytic work applicable at small $v$, higher-order \
corrections in\\nthe velocity are found to be important: the total force is \
nonzero, and the\\ndependence is not linear in $v$. We find that in all cases \
the total force is\\nin the opposite direction to the hydrodynamical \
analogue, although at low\\nspeeds it appears to approach the expectation \
that the Weyl and Magnus\\ncomponents cancel. Spin-curvature effects may \
leave an imprint on gravitational\\nwave signals from extreme mass-ratio \
inspirals, where the secondary black hole\\nhas a nonnegligible spin and \
moves in the presence of a dark matter cloud. We\\nhope that our simulations \
can be used to support and extend the limits of\\nanalytic results, which are \
necessary to better quantify such effects in the\\nrelativistic \
regime.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 RowBox[{"{", 
                   RowBox[{"{", 
                    RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"\"version\"", "\[Rule]", "\"v1\""}], ",", 
                    
                    RowBox[{
                    "\"created\"", "\[Rule]", 
                    "\"Mon, 12 Feb 2024 19:00:00 GMT\""}]}], "}"}], "}"}]}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"update_date\"", "\[Rule]", "\"2024-02-14\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"authors_parsed\"", "\[Rule]", 
