            "Zipeng Wang, Thomas Helfer, Dina Traykova, Katy Clough, Emanuele \
Berti", "title" -> "Gravitational Magnus effect from scalar dark matter", 
            "comments" -> "10 pages, 6 figures", "journal-ref" -> Null, "doi" -> 
            Null, "report-no" -> Null, "categories" -> "gr-qc astro-ph.HE", 
            "license" -> 
            "http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/", "abstract" -> 
            "  In fluid dynamics, the Magnus effect is the force \
perpendicular to the motion\nof a spinning object as it moves through a \
medium. In general relativity, an\nanalogous effect exists for a spinning \
compact object moving through matter,\npurely as a result of gravitational \
interactions. In this work we consider a\nKerr black hole moving at \
relativistic velocities through scalar dark matter\nthat is at rest. We \
simulate the system numerically and extract the total\nspin-curvature force \
on the black hole perpendicular to its motion. We confirm\nthat the force \
scales linearly with the dimensionless spin parameter $a/M$ of\nthe black \
hole up to $a/M = 0.99$, and measure its dependence on the speed $v$\nof the \
black hole in the range $0.1 \\le v \\le 0.55$ for a fixed spin. Compared\nto \
previous analytic work applicable at small $v$, higher-order corrections in\n\
the velocity are found to be important: the total force is nonzero, and the\n\
dependence is not linear in $v$. We find that in all cases the total force is\
\nin the opposite direction to the hydrodynamical analogue, although at low\n\
speeds it appears to approach the expectation that the Weyl and Magnus\n\
components cancel. Spin-curvature effects may leave an imprint on \
gravitational\nwave signals from extreme mass-ratio inspirals, where the \
secondary black hole\nhas a nonnegligible spin and moves in the presence of a \
dark matter cloud. We\nhope that our simulations can be used to support and \
extend the limits of\nanalytic results, which are necessary to better \
quantify such effects in the\nrelativistic regime.\n", 
