               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Modular invariance and thermal effective field theory in \
CFT\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"20 pages and a lotta circles. v2: updated \
references\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]},\
 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  We use thermal effective field theory to derive that \
the coefficient of the\\nfirst subleading piece of the thermal free energy, \
$c_1$, is equal to the\\ncoefficient of the subleading piece of the Casimir \
energy on $S^1 \\\\times\\nS^{d-2}$ for $d \\\\geq 4$. We conjecture that \
this coefficient obeys a sign\\nconstraint $c_1 \\\\geq 0$ in CFT and collect \
some evidence for this bound. We\\ndiscuss various applications of the \
thermal effective field theory, including\\nplacing the CFT on different \
spatial backgrounds and turning on chemical\\npotentials for $U(1)$ charge \
and angular momentum. Along the way, we derive the\\nhigh-temperature \
partition function on a sphere with arbitrary angular\\nvelocities using only \
time dilation and length contraction.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 RowBox[{"{", 
                   RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"{", 
