                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Signatures of ultralight bosons in the orbital \
eccentricity of binary\\n  black holes\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"v2: 5 pages + 11 pages appendices and references, 11 \
figures; few\\n  typos corrected; added two sections to the appendices; \
published in PRL\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Phys.Rev.Lett. 133 (2024) 12, 121401\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"10.1103/PhysRevLett.133.121401\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "\"DESY-24-030\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"gr-qc astro-ph.CO astro-ph.HE hep-ph hep-th\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  We show that the existence of clouds of ultralight \
particles surrounding\\nblack holes during their cosmological history as \
members of a binary system can\\nleave a measurable imprint on the \
distribution of masses and orbital\\neccentricities observable with future \
gravitational-wave detectors. Notably, we\\nfind that for nonprecessing \
binaries with chirp masses ${\\\\cal M} \\\\lesssim\\n10\\\\,M_\\\\odot$, \
formed exclusively in isolation, larger-than-expected values of\\nthe \
eccentricity, i.e. $e\\\\gtrsim 10^{-2}$ at gravitational-wave \
frequencies\\n$f_{\\\\rm GW} \\\\simeq 10^{-2}\\\\,$Hz, would provide \
tantalizing evidence for a new\\nparticle of mass between $[0.5,2.5] \
\\\\times 10^{-12}\\\\,$eV in nature. The\\npredicted evolution of the \
eccentricity can also drastically affect the in-band\\nphase evolution and \
peak frequency. These results constitute unique signatures\\nof boson clouds \
of ultralight particles in the dynamics of binary black holes,\\nwhich will \
be readily accessible with the Laser Interferometer Space Antenna,\\nas well \
as future mid-band and Deci-hertz detectors.\\n\""}]}, {
