            "Signatures of ultralight bosons in the orbital eccentricity of \
binary\n  black holes", "comments" -> 
            "v2: 5 pages + 11 pages appendices and references, 11 figures; \
few\n  typos corrected; added two sections to the appendices; published in \
PRL", "journal-ref" -> "Phys.Rev.Lett. 133 (2024) 12, 121401", "doi" -> 
            "10.1103/PhysRevLett.133.121401", "report-no" -> "DESY-24-030", 
            "categories" -> "gr-qc astro-ph.CO astro-ph.HE hep-ph hep-th", 
            "license" -> "http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/", 
            "abstract" -> 
            "  We show that the existence of clouds of ultralight particles \
surrounding\nblack holes during their cosmological history as members of a \
binary system can\nleave a measurable imprint on the distribution of masses \
and orbital\neccentricities observable with future gravitational-wave \
detectors. Notably, we\nfind that for nonprecessing binaries with chirp \
masses ${\\cal M} \\lesssim\n10\\,M_\\odot$, formed exclusively in isolation, \
larger-than-expected values of\nthe eccentricity, i.e. $e\\gtrsim 10^{-2}$ at \
gravitational-wave frequencies\n$f_{\\rm GW} \\simeq 10^{-2}\\,$Hz, would \
provide tantalizing evidence for a new\nparticle of mass between $[0.5,2.5] \
\\times 10^{-12}\\,$eV in nature. The\npredicted evolution of the \
eccentricity can also drastically affect the in-band\nphase evolution and \
peak frequency. These results constitute unique signatures\nof boson clouds \
of ultralight particles in the dynamics of binary black holes,\nwhich will be \
readily accessible with the Laser Interferometer Space Antenna,\nas well as \
future mid-band and Deci-hertz detectors.\n", 
            "versions" -> {{
