               RowBox[{"\"id\"", "\[Rule]", "\"2403.03147\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"submitter\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Giovanni Maria Tomaselli\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"authors\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Giovanni Maria Tomaselli, Thomas F.M. Spieksma, \
Gianfranco Bertone\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Resonant history of gravitational atoms in black hole \
binaries\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", "\"37+9 pages, 16 figures\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"gr-qc hep-ph hep-th\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]},\
 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  Rotating black holes can produce superradiant clouds of \
ultralight bosons.\\nWhen the black hole is part of a binary system, its \
cloud can undergo\\nresonances and ionization. These processes leave a \
distinct signature on the\\ngravitational waveform that depends on the \
cloud's properties. To determine the\\nstate of the cloud by the time the \
system enters the band of future millihertz\\ndetectors, we study the \
chronological sequence of resonances encountered during\\nthe inspiral. For \
the first time, we consistently take into account the\\nnonlinearities \
induced by the orbital backreaction and we allow the orbit to\\nhave generic \
eccentricity and inclination. We find that the resonance\\nphenomenology \
exhibits striking new features. Resonances can \\\"start\\\" or \\\"break\\\"\
\\nabove critical thresholds of the parameters, which we compute \
analytically, and\\ninduce dramatic changes in eccentricity and inclination. \
Applying these results\\nto realistic systems, we find two possible outcomes. \
If the binary and the\\ncloud are sufficiently close to counter-rotating, the \
cloud survives in its\\noriginal state until the system enters in band; \
otherwise, the cloud is\\ndestroyed during a resonance at large separations, \
but leaves an imprint on the\\neccentricity and inclination. In both \
scenarios, we characterize the\\nobservational signatures, with particular \
focus on future gravitational wave\\ndetectors.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
