            "Giovanni Maria Tomaselli, Thomas F.M. Spieksma, Gianfranco \
Bertone", "title" -> 
            "Resonant history of gravitational atoms in black hole binaries", 
            "comments" -> "37+9 pages, 16 figures", "journal-ref" -> Null, 
            "doi" -> Null, "report-no" -> Null, "categories" -> 
            "gr-qc hep-ph hep-th", "license" -> 
            "http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/", "abstract" -> 
            "  Rotating black holes can produce superradiant clouds of \
ultralight bosons.\nWhen the black hole is part of a binary system, its cloud \
can undergo\nresonances and ionization. These processes leave a distinct \
signature on the\ngravitational waveform that depends on the cloud's \
properties. To determine the\nstate of the cloud by the time the system \
enters the band of future millihertz\ndetectors, we study the chronological \
sequence of resonances encountered during\nthe inspiral. For the first time, \
we consistently take into account the\nnonlinearities induced by the orbital \
backreaction and we allow the orbit to\nhave generic eccentricity and \
inclination. We find that the resonance\nphenomenology exhibits striking new \
features. Resonances can \"start\" or \"break\"\nabove critical thresholds of \
the parameters, which we compute analytically, and\ninduce dramatic changes \
in eccentricity and inclination. Applying these results\nto realistic \
systems, we find two possible outcomes. If the binary and the\ncloud are \
sufficiently close to counter-rotating, the cloud survives in its\noriginal \
state until the system enters in band; otherwise, the cloud is\ndestroyed \
during a resonance at large separations, but leaves an imprint on the\n\
eccentricity and inclination. In both scenarios, we characterize the\n\
observational signatures, with particular focus on future gravitational wave\n\
detectors.\n", 
