               RowBox[{"\"id\"", "\[Rule]", "\"2404.11110\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"submitter\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Hector O. Silva\""}]}, {
              
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"authors\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Hector O. Silva, Giovanni Tambalo, Kostas Glampedakis, \
Kent Yagi, Jan\\n  Steinhoff\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Quasinormal modes and their excitation beyond general \
relativity\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"21 pages, 9 figures, 1 table. (v2) Matches version \
published in Phys.\\n  Rev. D\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "\"10.1103/PhysRevD.110.024042\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"gr-qc hep-th\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  The response of black holes to small perturbations is \
known to be partially\\ndescribed by a superposition of quasinormal modes. \
Despite their importance to\\nenable strong-field tests of gravity, little to \
nothing is known about what\\novertones and quasinormal-mode amplitudes are \
like for black holes in\\nextensions to general relativity. We take a first \
step in this direction and\\nstudy what is arguably the simplest model that \
allows first-principle\\ncalculations to be made: a nonrotating black hole in \
an effective-field-theory\\nextension of general relativity with \
cubic-in-curvature terms. Using a\\nphase-amplitude scheme that uses \
analytical continuation and the Pr\\\\\\\"ufer\\ntransformation, we compute, \
for the first time, the quasinormal overtone\\nfrequencies (in this theory) \
and quasinormal-mode excitation factors (in any\\ntheory beyond general \
relativity). We find that the overtone quasinormal\\nfrequencies and their \
excitation factors are more sensitive than the\\nfundamental mode to the \
lengthscale $l$ introduced by the higher-derivative\\nterms in the effective \
field theory. We argue that a description of all\\novertones cannot be made \
within the regime of validity of the effective field\\ntheory, and we \
conjecture that this is a general feature of any extension to\\ngeneral \
relativity that introduces a new lengthscale. We also find that \
a\\nparametrization of the modifications to the general-relativistic \
quasinormal\\nfrequencies in terms of the ratio between $l$ and the black \
hole's mass is\\nsomewhat inadequate, and we propose a better alternative. As \
an application, we\\nperform a preliminary study of the implications of the \
breakdown, in the\\neffective field theory, of the equivalence between the \
quasinormal mode spectra\\nassociated to metric perturbations of polar and \
axial parity of the\\nSchwarzschild black hole in general relativity. We also \
present a simple\\njustification for the loss of isospectrality.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
