            "http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/", "abstract" -> 
            "  The response of black holes to small perturbations is known to \
be partially\ndescribed by a superposition of quasinormal modes. Despite \
their importance to\nenable strong-field tests of gravity, little to nothing \
is known about what\novertones and quasinormal-mode amplitudes are like for \
black holes in\nextensions to general relativity. We take a first step in \
this direction and\nstudy what is arguably the simplest model that allows \
first-principle\ncalculations to be made: a nonrotating black hole in an \
effective-field-theory\nextension of general relativity with \
cubic-in-curvature terms. Using a\nphase-amplitude scheme that uses \
analytical continuation and the Pr\\\"ufer\ntransformation, we compute, for \
the first time, the quasinormal overtone\nfrequencies (in this theory) and \
quasinormal-mode excitation factors (in any\ntheory beyond general \
relativity). We find that the overtone quasinormal\nfrequencies and their \
excitation factors are more sensitive than the\nfundamental mode to the \
lengthscale $l$ introduced by the higher-derivative\nterms in the effective \
field theory. We argue that a description of all\novertones cannot be made \
within the regime of validity of the effective field\ntheory, and we \
conjecture that this is a general feature of any extension to\ngeneral \
relativity that introduces a new lengthscale. We also find that a\n\
parametrization of the modifications to the general-relativistic quasinormal\n\
frequencies in terms of the ratio between $l$ and the black hole's mass is\n\
somewhat inadequate, and we propose a better alternative. As an application, \
we\nperform a preliminary study of the implications of the breakdown, in the\n\
effective field theory, of the equivalence between the quasinormal mode \
spectra\nassociated to metric perturbations of polar and axial parity of the\n\
Schwarzschild black hole in general relativity. We also present a simple\n\
justification for the loss of isospectrality.\n", 
