         Annotation[#, 
          
          TableForm[{
           "id" -> "2405.13442", "submitter" -> "Lorenzo Brevi", "authors" -> 
            "Lorenzo Brevi, Antonio Mandarino, and Enrico Prati", "title" -> 
            "Addressing the Non-perturbative Regime of the Quantum Anharmonic\
\n  Oscillator by Physics-Informed Neural Networks", "comments" -> 
            "18 pages, 10 figures. Comments are welcome", "journal-ref" -> 
            Null, "doi" -> Null, "report-no" -> Null, "categories" -> 
            "quant-ph hep-th", "license" -> 
            "http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/", "abstract" -> 
            "  The use of deep learning in physical sciences has recently \
boosted the\nability of researchers to tackle physical systems where little \
or no analytical\ninsight is available. Recently, the Physics-Informed Neural \
Networks (PINNs)\nhave been introduced as one of the most promising tools to \
solve systems of\ndifferential equations guided by some physically grounded \
constraints. In the\nquantum realm, such approach paves the way to a novel \
approach to solve the\nSchroedinger equation for non-integrable systems. By \
following an unsupervised\nlearning approach, we apply the PINNs to the \
anharmonic oscillator in which an\ninteraction term proportional to the \
fourth power of the position coordinate is\npresent. We compute the \
eigenenergies and the corresponding eigenfunctions\nwhile varying the weight \
of the quartic interaction. We bridge our solutions to\nthe regime where both \
the perturbative and the strong coupling theory work,\nincluding the pure \
quartic oscillator. We investigate systems with real and\nimaginary \
frequency, laying the foundation for novel numerical methods to\ntackle \
problems emerging in quantum field theory.\n", 
            "versions" -> {{
              "version" -> "v1", "created" -> 
               "Wed, 22 May 2024 08:34:52 GMT"}, {
              "version" -> "v2", "created" -> 
               "Wed, 11 Sep 2024 11:32:29 GMT"}}, "update_date" -> 
            "2024-09-12", 
            "authors_parsed" -> {{"Brevi", "Lorenzo", ""}, {
              "Mandarino", "Antonio", ""}, {"Prati", "Enrico", ""}}}], 
          "Tooltip"]& ], 
        TagBox[
         TooltipBox[
          DiskBox[{3.1200856993201334`, 1.9370644872619245`}, 
           0.08283829248930569],
          TagBox[
           TagBox[
            GridBox[{{
               RowBox[{"\"id\"", "\[Rule]", "\"2405.13826\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"submitter\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Pablo Vives Matasan\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"authors\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Andreas Brandhuber, Graham R. Brown, Paolo Pichini, \
Gabriele\\n  Travaglini, Pablo Vives Matasan\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Spinning binary dynamics in cubic effective field \
theories of gravity\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"49 pages; v2: typos corrected, references added, JHEP \
version\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "\"QMUL-PH-24-09\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th gr-qc\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  We study the binary dynamics of two Kerr black holes \
with arbitrary spin\\nvectors in the presence of parity-even and parity-odd \
cubic deformations of\\ngravity. We first derive the tree-level Compton \
amplitudes for a Kerr black\\nhole in cubic gravity, which we then use to \
compute the two-to-two amplitudes\\nof the massive bodies to leading order in \
the deformation and the\\npost-Minkowskian expansion. The required one-loop \
computations are performed\\nusing the leading singularity approach as well \
as the heavy-mass effective\\nfield theory (HEFT) approach. These amplitudes \
are then used to compute the\\nleading-order momentum and spin kick in cubic \
gravity in the KMOC formalism.\\nOur results are valid for generic masses and \
spin vectors, and include all the\\nindependent parity-even and parity-odd \
cubic deformations of Einstein-Hilbert\\ngravity. We also present \
spin-expanded expressions for the momentum and spin\\nkicks, and the \
all-order in spin deflection angle in the case of aligned spins.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
