           "id" -> "2405.15219", "submitter" -> "Piyabut Burikham", "authors" -> 
            "David Senjaya, Piyabut Burikham, Tiberiu Harko", "title" -> 
            "The Exact Relativistic Scalar Quasibound States of The Dyonic \
Kerr-Sen\n  Black Hole: Quantized Energy, and Hawking Radiation", "comments" -> 
            "18 pages, 9 figures", "journal-ref" -> Null, "doi" -> Null, 
            "report-no" -> Null, "categories" -> "gr-qc hep-th", "license" -> 
            "http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/", "abstract" -> 
            "  We consider Klein-Gordon equation in the Dyonic Kerr-Sen black \
hole\nbackground, which is the charged rotating axially symmetric solution of \
the\nEinstein-Maxwell-Dilaton-Axion theory of gravity. The black hole \
incorporates\nelectric, magnetic, dilatonic and axionic charges and is \
constructed in 3+1\ndimensional spacetime. We begin our investigations with \
the construction of the\nscalar field's governing equation, i.e., the \
covariant Klein-Gordon equation.\nWith the help of the ansatz of separation \
of variables, we successfully\nseparate the polar part, and find the exact \
solution in terms of Spheroidal\nHarmonics, while the radial exact solution \
is obtained in terms of the\nConfluent Heun function. The quantization of the \
quasibound state is done by\napplying the polynomial condition of the \
Confluent Heun function that gives\nrise to discrete complex-valued energy \
levels for massive scalar fields. The\nreal part is the scalar field \
relativistic quantized energy, while the\nimaginary part represents the \
quasibound states's decay. We present all of the\nsixteen possible exact \
energy solutions for both massive and massless scalars.\nWe also present the \
investigation the Hawking radiation of the Dyonic Kerr-Sen\nblack hole's \
apparent horizon, via the Sigurd-Sannan method by making use of\nthe obtained \
exact scalar wave functions. The radiation distribution function,\nand the \
Hawking temperature are successfully obtained.\n", 
            "versions" -> {{
              "version" -> "v1", "created" -> 
               "Fri, 24 May 2024 05:18:42 GMT"}, {
              "version" -> "v2", "created" -> 
               "Sat, 10 Aug 2024 14:53:12 GMT"}}, "update_date" -> 
            "2024-08-13", 
