                RowBox[{"\"id\"", "\[Rule]", "\"2406.07458\""}]}, {
                RowBox[{"\"submitter\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Kuo-Wei Huang\""}]}, {
                RowBox[{"\"authors\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Kuo-Wei Huang\""}]}, {
                
                RowBox[{
                 "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                  "\"Resummation of Multi-Stress Tensors in Higher Dimensions\
\""}]}, {
                
                RowBox[{
                 "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", 
                  "\"39 pages, v2: notation improved\""}]}, {
                RowBox[{"\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
                RowBox[{"\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
                RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
                RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th\""}]}, {
                
                RowBox[{
                 "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                  "\"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/\""}]}, {
                
                RowBox[{
                 "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                  
                  "\"  In the context of holographic conformal field theories \
(CFTs), a system of\\nlinear partial differential equations was recently \
proposed to be the\\nhigher-dimensional analog of the null-state equations in \
$d=2$ CFTs at large\\ncentral charge. Solving these equations in a \
near-lightcone expansion yields\\nsolutions that match the minimal-twist \
multi-stress tensor contributions to a\\nheavy-light four-point correlator \
(or a thermal two-point correlator) computed\\nusing holography, the \
conformal bootstrap, and other methods. This note\\nexplores the exact \
solutions to these equations. We begin by observing that, in\\nan expansion \
in terms of the ratio between the heavy operator's dimension and\\nthe \
central charge, the $d=2$ correlator involving the level-two \
degenerate\\nscalars at each order can be represented as a Bessel function; \
the resummation\\nyields the Virasoro vacuum block. We next observe a \
relation between the $d=2$\\ncorrelator and the $d=4$ near-lightcone \
correlator involving light scalars with\\nthe same conformal dimension. The \
resummed $d=4$ correlator takes a simple form\\nin the complex frequency \
domain. Unlike the Virasoro vacuum block, the\\nresummation in $d=4$ leads to \
essential singularities. Similar expressions are\\nalso obtained when the \
light scalar's dimension takes other finite values.\\nThese CFT results \
correspond to a holographic computation with a spherical\\nblack hole. In \
addition, using the differential equations, we demonstrate that\\nthe \
correlators can be reconstructed via certain modes. In $d=2$, these \
modes\\nare related to the Virasoro algebra.\\n\""}]}, {
                RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
                  RowBox[{"{", 
                    RowBox[{
