            "id" -> "2406.07458", "submitter" -> "Kuo-Wei Huang", "authors" -> 
             "Kuo-Wei Huang", "title" -> 
             "Resummation of Multi-Stress Tensors in Higher Dimensions", 
             "comments" -> "39 pages, v2: notation improved", "journal-ref" -> 
             Null, "doi" -> Null, "report-no" -> Null, "categories" -> 
             "hep-th", "license" -> 
             "http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/", "abstract" -> 
             "  In the context of holographic conformal field theories \
(CFTs), a system of\nlinear partial differential equations was recently \
proposed to be the\nhigher-dimensional analog of the null-state equations in \
$d=2$ CFTs at large\ncentral charge. Solving these equations in a \
near-lightcone expansion yields\nsolutions that match the minimal-twist \
multi-stress tensor contributions to a\nheavy-light four-point correlator (or \
a thermal two-point correlator) computed\nusing holography, the conformal \
bootstrap, and other methods. This note\nexplores the exact solutions to \
these equations. We begin by observing that, in\nan expansion in terms of the \
ratio between the heavy operator's dimension and\nthe central charge, the \
$d=2$ correlator involving the level-two degenerate\nscalars at each order \
can be represented as a Bessel function; the resummation\nyields the Virasoro \
vacuum block. We next observe a relation between the $d=2$\ncorrelator and \
the $d=4$ near-lightcone correlator involving light scalars with\nthe same \
conformal dimension. The resummed $d=4$ correlator takes a simple form\nin \
the complex frequency domain. Unlike the Virasoro vacuum block, the\n\
resummation in $d=4$ leads to essential singularities. Similar expressions \
are\nalso obtained when the light scalar's dimension takes other finite \
values.\nThese CFT results correspond to a holographic computation with a \
spherical\nblack hole. In addition, using the differential equations, we \
demonstrate that\nthe correlators can be reconstructed via certain modes. In \
$d=2$, these modes\nare related to the Virasoro algebra.\n", 
             "versions" -> {{
               "version" -> "v1", "created" -> 
