          TableForm[{
           "id" -> "2406.10879", "submitter" -> "Suchetan Das", "authors" -> 
            "Suchetan Das", "title" -> 
            "Stretched Horizon from Conformal Field Theory", "comments" -> 
            "28 pages, one appendix", "journal-ref" -> Null, "doi" -> Null, 
            "report-no" -> Null, "categories" -> "hep-th", "license" -> 
            "http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/", "abstract" -> 
            "  Recently, it has been observed that the Hartle-Hawking \
correlators, a\nsignature of smooth horizon, can emerge from certain heavy \
excited state\ncorrelators in the (manifestly non-smooth) BTZ stretched \
horizon background, in\nthe limit when the stretched horizon approaches the \
real horizon. In this note,\nwe develop a framework of quantizing the CFT \
modular Hamiltonian, that explains\nthe necessity of introducing a stretched \
horizon and the emergence of thermal\nfeatures in the AdS-Rindler and \
(planar) BTZ backgrounds. In more detail, we\nquantize vacuum modular \
Hamiltonian on a spatial segment of $S^{1}$. Unlike\nradial quantization, \
(Euclidean) time circles emerge naturally here which can\nbe contracted \
smoothly to the `fixed points'(end points of the interval) of\nthis \
quantization thus providing a direct link to thermal physics. To define a\n\
Hilbert space with discrete normalizable states and to construct a Virasoro\n\
algebra with finite central extension, a natural regulator ($\\epsilon$) is\n\
needed around the fixed points. Eventually, in the dual description the fixed\
\npoints correspond to the horizons of AdS-Rindler patch or (planar) BTZ and \
the\ncut-off being the stretched horizon. We construct a (Lorentzian) highest \
weight\nrepresentation of that Virasoro algebra. We further demonstrate that \
two point\nfunction in a (vacuum) descendant state of the regulated Hilbert \
space will\nreproduce thermal answer in $\\epsilon \\rightarrow 0$ limit \
which is analogous\nto the recent observation of emergent thermality in \
stretched horizon\nbackground. We also argue the thermal entropy of this \
quantization coincides\nwith entanglement entropy of the subregion. \
Conversely, the microcanonical\nentropy corresponding to high energy density \
of states exactly reproduce the\nBTZ entropy. Quite remarkably, all these \
dominant high lying microstates are\ndefined only at finite $\\epsilon$ in \
the regulated Hilbert space.\n", 
