 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  Scalar field theories with a shift symmetry come \
equipped with the\\n$K$-mouflage (or kinetic screening) mechanism that \
suppresses the scalar\\ninteraction between massive objects below a certain \
distance, the screening\\nradius. In this work, we study the linear response \
of the scalar field\\ndistribution around a screened (point-like) object \
subject to a long range\\nexternal scalar field perturbation for the \
Dirac-Born-Infeld theory. We find\\nthat, for regular boundary conditions at \
the position of the particle, some\\nmultipoles have vanishing response for a \
lacunar series of the multipole order\\n$\\\\ell$ for any dimension. Some \
multipoles also exhibit a logarithmic running\\nwhen the number of spatial \
dimensions is even. We construct a ladder operator\\nstructure, with its \
associated ladder symmetries, formed by two sets of ladders\\nthat are \
related to the properties of the linear response and the existence \
of\\nconserved charges. Our results exhibit a remarkable resemblance with the \
Love\\nnumbers properties of black holes in General Relativity, although some\
\\nintriguing differences subsist.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 RowBox[{"{", 
                   RowBox[{"{", 
