          TagBox[
           TagBox[
            GridBox[{{
               RowBox[{"\"id\"", "\[Rule]", "\"2407.11952\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"submitter\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Chethan Krishnan\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"authors\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Chethan Krishnan, Ranjini Mondol\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Young Black Holes Have Smooth Horizons: A Swampland \
Argument\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", "\"16 pages, 16 plots\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th gr-qc\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]},\
 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  It has been suggested that black hole microstates in \
string theory may have\\n\\\"no interiors\\\". The arguments of \
arXiv:2312.14108 suggest that even if this\\nwere the case, conventional bulk \
EFT should be valid at the horizon for black\\nholes that are not too old. \
Here, we explore the validity of bulk EFT for young\\nblack holes from a \
different perspective, by considering the gravitational\\ncollapse of scalar \
moduli. This builds on arXiv:2003.05488, which studied the\\nnumerical \
collapse of a scalar field in a Choptuik-like set up. Evidence \
was\\npresented there for two observations -- (a) For every scalar profile \
that\\nresults in collapse, the scalar field undergoes trans-Planckian \
variation, (b)\\nEvery trans-Planckian scalar motion is hidden behind an \
apparent horizon. The\\nresults of arXiv:2003.05488 were mostly obtained in \
regimes close to critical\\ncollapse, where scale-separation was difficult to \
achieve. In this paper, we\\nstudy the same system in super-critical regimes. \
This allows us to study the\\nlocalization properties of scalar variations \
inside the apparent horizon by\\nseparating the horizon and singularity \
scales. We find strong evidence that the\\n$\\\\mathcal{O}(1)$ scalar \
variation is localized around the curvature divergence,\\nand is \
hierarchically well-separated from the apparent horizon. \
Because\\n$\\\\mathcal{O}(1)$ scalar movement is associated to the breakdown \
of bulk EFT\\naccording to the swampland distance conjecture, this is an \
indication that bulk\\nEFT is well-behaved in the interiors of young black \
holes. Our results suggest\\nthe perspective that cosmic censorship is a \
mechanism for preserving bulk EFT\\nbetween the horizon and the \
singularity.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 RowBox[{"{", 
                   RowBox[{"{", 
                    RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"\"version\"", "\[Rule]", "\"v1\""}], ",", 
                    
                    RowBox[{
                    "\"created\"", "\[Rule]", 
                    "\"Tue, 16 Jul 2024 17:46:03 GMT\""}]}], "}"}], "}"}]}]}, {
