            GridBox[{{
               RowBox[{"\"id\"", "\[Rule]", "\"2407.13385\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"submitter\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Weitao Ye\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"authors\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Zhaofeng Kang, Tianjun Li and Weitao Ye\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"The Stochastic Gravitational Wave Background from \
Primordial\\n  Gravitational Atoms\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-ph astro-ph.CO\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]},\
 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  We propose a scenario of primordial gravitational atoms \
(PGAs), which may\\nexist in the current and past universe due to spinning \
primordial black holes\\n(PBHs) and very light bosonic fields. In a \
monochromatic mass scenario with a\\nsizable dimensionless spin, which may \
arise in a short matter dominated (MD)\\nera, we analyze the resulting \
stochastic gravitational wave background (SGWB)\\nsignal. Its spectrum is \
approximately characterized by a rising $\\\\propto f^3$\\nfollowed by a \
falling $\\\\propto f^{-1}$ where $f$ is the frequency. Then, \
we\\ninvestigate the constraints and prospects of such a SGWB, and find that \
PGAs\\nwith a core mass $M_{\\\\rm BH}\\\\sim {\\\\cal O}(10)~M_{\\\\odot}$ \
and a cloud of light\\nscalar with mass $\\\\mu \\\\sim {\\\\cal O} \
(10^{-13})$ eV could yield constraints even\\nstronger than those from bare \
PBHs. Future detectors such as LISA, Taiji and\\nTianQin are able to explore \
PGAs over a narrow and elongated strap in the\\n$(\\\\mu,M_{\\\\rm BH})$ \
plane, spanning over 10 orders of magnitude for the maximum\\nspin, \
$10^{-8}~M_{\\\\odot}\\\\lesssim M_{\\\\rm BH}\\\\lesssim 10^4~M_{\\\\odot}$,\
\\n$10^{-16}~{\\\\rm eV}\\\\lesssim \\\\mu\\\\lesssim 10^{-3}~\\\\rm eV$. If \
the PGA is dressed\\nwith a vector cloud, the SGWB signal has a much better \
opportunity to be\\nprobed.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 RowBox[{"{", 
                   RowBox[{"{", 
                    RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"\"version\"", "\[Rule]", "\"v1\""}], ",", 
                    
