            GridBox[{{
               RowBox[{"\"id\"", "\[Rule]", "\"2407.19005\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"submitter\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Mao Zeng\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"authors\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Dogan Akpinar, Fernando Febres Cordero, Manfred Kraus, \
Michael S. Ruf,\\n  Mao Zeng\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Spinning Black Hole Scattering at $\\\\mathcal{O}(G^3 \
S^2)$: Casimir Terms,\\n  Radial Action and Hidden Symmetry\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", "\"45 pages, 3 figures\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th gr-qc\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  We resolve subtleties in calculating the \
post-Minksowskian dynamics of binary\\nsystems, as a spin expansion, from \
massive scattering amplitudes of fixed\\nfinite spin. In particular, the \
apparently ambiguous spin Casimir terms can be\\nfully determined from the \
gradient of the spin-diagonal part of the amplitudes\\nwith respect to $S^2 = \
-s(s+1)\\\\hbar^2$, using an interpolation between massive\\namplitudes with \
different spin representations. From two-loop amplitudes of\\nspin-0 and \
spin-1 particles minimally coupled to gravity, we extract the spin\\nCasimir \
terms in the conservative scattering angle between a spinless and \
a\\nspinning black hole at $\\\\mathcal{O}(G^3 S^2)$, finding agreement with \
known\\nresults in the literature. This completes an earlier study [Phys. \
Rev. Lett.\\n130 (2023), 021601] that calculated the non-Casimir terms from \
amplitudes. We\\nalso illustrate our methods using a model of spinning bodies \
in\\nelectrodynamics, finding agreement between scattering amplitude \
predictions and\\nclassical predictions in a root-Kerr electromagnetic \
background up to\\n$\\\\mathcal{O}(\\\\alpha^3 S^2)$. For both gravity and \
electrodynamics, the finite\\npart of the amplitude coincides with the \
two-body radial action in the aligned\\nspin limit, generalizing the \
amplitude-action relation beyond the spinless\\ncase. Surprisingly, the \
two-loop amplitude displays a hidden spin-shift\\nsymmetry in the probe \
limit, which was previously observed at one loop. We\\nconjecture that the \
symmetry holds to all orders in the coupling constant and\\nis a consequence \
of integrability of Kerr orbits in the probe limit at the\\nfirst few orders \
in spin.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 RowBox[{"{", 
                   RowBox[{"{", 
                    RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"\"version\"", "\[Rule]", "\"v1\""}], ",", 
                    
                    RowBox[{
                    "\"created\"", "\[Rule]", 
                    "\"Fri, 26 Jul 2024 18:00:01 GMT\""}]}], "}"}], "}"}]}]}, {
