          TableForm[{
           "id" -> "2407.19005", "submitter" -> "Mao Zeng", "authors" -> 
            "Dogan Akpinar, Fernando Febres Cordero, Manfred Kraus, Michael \
S. Ruf,\n  Mao Zeng", "title" -> 
            "Spinning Black Hole Scattering at $\\mathcal{O}(G^3 S^2)$: \
Casimir Terms,\n  Radial Action and Hidden Symmetry", "comments" -> 
            "45 pages, 3 figures", "journal-ref" -> Null, "doi" -> Null, 
            "report-no" -> Null, "categories" -> "hep-th gr-qc", "license" -> 
            "http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/", "abstract" -> 
            "  We resolve subtleties in calculating the post-Minksowskian \
dynamics of binary\nsystems, as a spin expansion, from massive scattering \
amplitudes of fixed\nfinite spin. In particular, the apparently ambiguous \
spin Casimir terms can be\nfully determined from the gradient of the \
spin-diagonal part of the amplitudes\nwith respect to $S^2 = \
-s(s+1)\\hbar^2$, using an interpolation between massive\namplitudes with \
different spin representations. From two-loop amplitudes of\nspin-0 and \
spin-1 particles minimally coupled to gravity, we extract the spin\nCasimir \
terms in the conservative scattering angle between a spinless and a\nspinning \
black hole at $\\mathcal{O}(G^3 S^2)$, finding agreement with known\nresults \
in the literature. This completes an earlier study [Phys. Rev. Lett.\n130 \
(2023), 021601] that calculated the non-Casimir terms from amplitudes. We\n\
also illustrate our methods using a model of spinning bodies in\n\
electrodynamics, finding agreement between scattering amplitude predictions \
and\nclassical predictions in a root-Kerr electromagnetic background up to\n$\
\\mathcal{O}(\\alpha^3 S^2)$. For both gravity and electrodynamics, the \
finite\npart of the amplitude coincides with the two-body radial action in \
the aligned\nspin limit, generalizing the amplitude-action relation beyond \
the spinless\ncase. Surprisingly, the two-loop amplitude displays a hidden \
spin-shift\nsymmetry in the probe limit, which was previously observed at one \
loop. We\nconjecture that the symmetry holds to all orders in the coupling \
constant and\nis a consequence of integrability of Kerr orbits in the probe \
limit at the\nfirst few orders in spin.\n", 
            "versions" -> {{
              "version" -> "v1", "created" -> 
