, {
                
                RowBox[{
                 "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                  "\"  Accurate modeling of compact binaries is essential for \
gravitational-wave\\ndetection and parameter estimation, with spin being an \
important effect to\\ninclude in waveform models. In this paper, we derive \
new post-Newtonian (PN)\\nresults for the conservative aligned-spin dynamics \
at next-to-next-to-leading\\norder for the spin$^3$ and spin$^4$ \
contributions, in addition to the\\nnext-to-leading order (NLO) spin$^5$ and \
spin$^6$ contributions. One approach\\nwe follow is the Tutti Frutti method, \
which relates PN and gravitational\\nself-force (GSF) results through the \
redshift and spin-precession invariants,\\nby making use of the simple \
dependence of the scattering angle on the symmetric\\nmass ratio. However, an \
ambiguity arises at the NLO spin$^5$ contribution, due\\nto transcendental \
functions of the Kerr spin in the redshift; this is also the\\norder at which \
Compton amplitudes calculations are affected by spurious poles.\\nTherefore, \
we follow an additional approach to determine the NLO spin$^5$ and\\nspin$^6$ \
dynamics: using on-shell Compton amplitudes obtained from black \
hole\\nperturbation theory. The Compton amplitude used in this work is \
composed of the\\nunambiguous tree-level far-zone part reported in [Phys. \
Rev. D 109, no.8,\\n084071 (2024)], as well as the full, non-interfering with \
the far-zone,\\n$\\\\ell=2$ partial wave contributions from the near zone, \
which are responsible\\nfor capturing Kerr finite-size effects. Other results \
in this paper include\\nderiving the scattering angle of a spinning test body \
in a Kerr background from\\na parametrized worldline action, and computing \
the redshift and spin-precession\\ninvariants for eccentric orbits without an \
eccentricity expansion.\\n\""}]}, {
                RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
                  RowBox[{"{", 
                    RowBox[{"{", 
                    RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"\"version\"", "\[Rule]", "\"v1\""}], ",", 
                    
