             "license" -> 
             "http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/", 
             "abstract" -> 
             "  Motivated by its relevance for thermal correlators in \
strongly coupled\nholographic CFTs, we refine and further develop a recent \
exact analytic\napproach to black hole perturbation problem, based on the \
semiclassical\nVirasoro blocks, or equivalently via AGT relation, the \
Nekrasov partition\nfunctions in the Nekrasov-Shatashvili limit. Focusing on \
asymptotically\n$\\text{AdS}_5$ black hole backgrounds, we derive new \
universal exact\nexpressions for holographic thermal two-point functions, \
both for scalar\noperators and conserved currents. Relatedly, we also obtain \
exact quantization\nconditions of the associated quasinormal modes (QNMs). \
Our expressions for the\nholographic $\\text{CFT}_4$ closely resemble the \
well-known results for 2d\nthermal CFTs on $\\mathbb{R}^{1,1}$. This \
structural similarity stems from the\nlocality of fusion transformation for \
Virasoro blocks. We provide numerical\nchecks of our quantization conditions \
for QNMs. Additionally, we discuss the\napplication of our results to \
understand specific physical properties of QNMs,\nincluding their \
near-extremal and asymptotic limits. The latter is related to a\ncertain \
large-momentum regime of semiclassical Virasoro blocks dual to\n\
Seiberg-Witten prepotentials.\n", 
             "versions" -> {{
               "version" -> "v1", "created" -> 
                "Fri, 9 Aug 2024 17:58:46 GMT"}}, "update_date" -> 
             "2024-08-12", 
             "authors_parsed" -> {{"Jia", "Hewei Frederic", ""}, {
               "Rangamani", "Mukund", ""}}}], "Tooltip"]& ]}, 
        TagBox[
         TooltipBox[
          DiskBox[{7.239397841352544, 1.6090118243307128`}, 
           0.08283829248930569],
          TagBox[
           TagBox[
            GridBox[{{
               RowBox[{"\"id\"", "\[Rule]", "\"2408.05569\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"submitter\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Di Wu\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"authors\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Wentao Liu, Di Wu, Jieci Wang\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Light rings and shadows of static black holes in \
effective quantum\\n  gravity\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"9 pages, 8 figures, revtex4-1.cls. arXiv admin note: text \
overlap\\n  with arXiv:2407.07416\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"gr-qc hep-th\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]},\
 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  Recently, two types of static black hole models that \
retain general\\ncovariance have been proposed within the Hamiltonian \
constraint approach to\\neffective quantum gravity (EQG). We have studied the \
light rings and shadows of\\nthese black holes using the topological method \
and the backward ray-tracing\\nmethod, respectively. We demonstrate that \
these light rings in both types of\\nstatic black holes are standard and \
unstable according to the classification of\\nlight rings. Subsequently, we \
checked the position of the light rings using the\\nphoton trajectory \
equation. We found that although the quantum parameters do\\nnot affect the \
light rings of these two types of black holes, they do reduce\\nthe size of \
the first type of static black hole in EQG, making it smaller.\\nHowever, for \
the second type of static black hole in EQG, we cannot distinguish\\nit from \
a Schwarzschild black hole based on the shadow alone. Fortunately, \
the\\nquantum parameters shrink the lensing rings of both types of black \
holes in\\nEQG, causing the black hole shadow to occupy a larger proportion \
within the\\nring. This can serve as a basis for distinguishing whether the \
black hole is in\\nEQG or general relativity (GR).\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
