            "Light rings and shadows of static black holes in effective \
quantum\n  gravity", "comments" -> 
            "9 pages, 8 figures, revtex4-1.cls. arXiv admin note: text \
overlap\n  with arXiv:2407.07416", "journal-ref" -> Null, "doi" -> Null, 
            "report-no" -> Null, "categories" -> "gr-qc hep-th", "license" -> 
            "http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/", "abstract" -> 
            "  Recently, two types of static black hole models that retain \
general\ncovariance have been proposed within the Hamiltonian constraint \
approach to\neffective quantum gravity (EQG). We have studied the light rings \
and shadows of\nthese black holes using the topological method and the \
backward ray-tracing\nmethod, respectively. We demonstrate that these light \
rings in both types of\nstatic black holes are standard and unstable \
according to the classification of\nlight rings. Subsequently, we checked the \
position of the light rings using the\nphoton trajectory equation. We found \
that although the quantum parameters do\nnot affect the light rings of these \
two types of black holes, they do reduce\nthe size of the first type of \
static black hole in EQG, making it smaller.\nHowever, for the second type of \
static black hole in EQG, we cannot distinguish\nit from a Schwarzschild \
black hole based on the shadow alone. Fortunately, the\nquantum parameters \
shrink the lensing rings of both types of black holes in\nEQG, causing the \
black hole shadow to occupy a larger proportion within the\nring. This can \
serve as a basis for distinguishing whether the black hole is in\nEQG or \
general relativity (GR).\n", 
