            "hep-th astro-ph.CO gr-qc", "license" -> 
            "http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/", "abstract" -> 
            "  If a set of massive objects collide in space and the fragments \
disperse,\npossibly forming black holes, then this process will emit \
gravitational waves.\nComputing the detailed gravitational wave-form \
associated with this process is\na complicated problem, not only due to the \
non-linearity of gravity but also\ndue to the fact that during the collision \
and subsequent fragmentation the\nobjects could undergo complicated \
non-gravitational interactions. Nevertheless\nthe classical soft graviton \
theorem determines the power law fall-off of the\nwave-form at late and early \
times, including logarithmic corrections, in terms\nof only the momenta of \
the incoming and outgoing objects without any reference\nto what transpired \
during the collision. In this short review I shall explain\nthe results and \
very briefly outline the derivation of these results.\n", 
