            "  Black hole perturbation theory on spherically symmetric \
backgrounds has been\ninstrumental in establishing various aspects about the \
gravitational dynamics\nclose to black holes, and continues to be an \
interesting avenue to confront\ncurrent challenges in gravitational physics. \
In this paper, we present an\napproach to perturbation theory in spherical \
symmetry that addresses\nsimultaneously some conceivably inconvenient aspects \
of the traditional\nmethods. In particular, focusing on Schwarzschild's \
background we are able to\nderive a decoupled wave equation, for a single \
complex variable, by simply\ncomputing one component of the curvature wave \
equation satisfied by a complex\nself-dual version of the Riemann tensor. The \
real and imaginary parts of the\nvariable consist only of even and odd pieces \
of the metric fluctuation,\nrespectively, and both satisfy the Regge-Wheeler \
equation. Besides providing a\nsystematic derivation of decoupled equations, \
an immediate corollary of our\nresults is the isospectrality between even and \
odd sectors. We conclude by\ndiscussing potential extensions of our formalism \
to include matter and higher\norders in perturbation theory.\n", 
