               RowBox[{
                "\"authors\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Elena C\\\\'aceres, \\\\'Angel J. Murcia, Ayan K. Patra \
and Juan F. Pedraza\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"Kasner eons with matter: holographic excursions to the \
black hole\\n  singularity\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"29 pages (including two appendices), 7 figures; v2: \
references added\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"WI-27-2024, IFT-UAM/CSIC-24-123\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"hep-th gr-qc\""}]}, {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 
                 "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]},\
 {
               
               RowBox[{
                "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                 "\"  Recent work has shown that introducing higher-curvature \
terms to the\\nEinstein-Hilbert action causes the approach to a space-like \
singularity to\\nunfold as a sequence of Kasner eons. Each eon is dominated \
by emergent physics\\nat an energy scale controlled by higher-curvature terms \
of a given order,\\ntransitioning to higher-order eons as the singularity is \
approached. The\\npurpose of this paper is twofold. First, we demonstrate \
that the inclusion of\\nmatter dramatically modifies the physics of eons \
compared to the vacuum case.\\nWe illustrate this by considering a family of \
quasi-topological gravities of\\narbitrary order minimally coupled to a \
scalar field. Second, we investigate\\nKasner eons in the interior of black \
holes with field theory duals and analyze\\ntheir imprints on holographic \
observables. We show that the behavior of the\\nthermal $a$-function, \
two-point functions of heavy operators, and holographic\\ncomplexity can \
capture distinct signatures of the eons, making them promising\\ntools for \
diagnosing stringy effects near black hole singularities.\\n\""}]}, {
               RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
